%N A087102 Number of numbers occurring most frequently as difference between consecutive primes <= prime(n).
%C A087102 For small n: a(n)<=3; A087103(n) and A087104(n) give the smallest and greatest jumping champion(s) for prime(n).
%C A087102 a(n) equals one of 1, a(n-1), or a(n-1)+1. - _Pontus von Brömssen_, Aug 14 2026
%H A087102 T. D. Noe, <a href="/A087102/b087102.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 2..1001</a>
%H A087102 A. Odlyzko, M. Rubinstein and M. Wolf, <a href="https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~odlyzko/doc/jumping.champions.pdf">Jumping Champions</a>.
%H A087102 A. Odlyzko, M. Rubinstein and M. Wolf, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10586458.1999.10504393">Jumping Champions</a>, Experimental Math., 8 (no. 2) (1999), 107-118; <a href="https://projecteuclid.org/journals/experimental-mathematics/volume-8/issue-2/Jumping-champions/em/1047477055.full">alternative link at Project Euclid</a>.
%H A087102 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/JumpingChampion.html">Jumping Champion</a>.
%t A087102 d=Table[0, {100}]; p=2; Table[q=NextPrime[p]; d[[q-p]]++; p=q; Length[Position[d, Max[d]]], {1000}]
%Y A087102 Cf. A001223, A005250, A087103, A087104, A398886.
%K A087102 nonn
%O A087102 2,2
